WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.610
Deadpool didn't just shake up the status quo
for action adventures starring guys in capes

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:09.880
it went the extra mile and set a lot of cinematic
precedents, some of them quite subtly. if

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:10.380
you can believe it.

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:13.980
This is how Deadpool changed movies and you
barely noticed.

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:18.660
Deadpool's marketing is basically a movie's
worth of entertainment in and of itself.

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:23.960
Seriously, from Mother's Day gags to billboards
that shouldn't exist, the folks behind Deadpool

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:28.200
pulled out all the stops to make sure hype
would be at its absolute peak by the time

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:29.820
the superhero hit the big screen.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:36.460
What made it so special isn't just its excessive,
over-the-top humor, but also its not-so-subtle

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:37.300
meta slant.

00:00:37.809 --> 00:00:42.460
Befitting the character, Deadpool's marketing
made sure to tap into fourth-wall-breaking

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:46.840
territory, showcasing a degree of self-awareness
that elated the meme generation.

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:50.260
"You're probably thinking, 'My boyfriend said
this was a superhero movie.'

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:53.580
Well, surprise: This is actually lucky you
a love story."

00:00:53.649 --> 00:00:58.920
This movie knew its target demographic so
well that it transcended its own status, resulting

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:03.520
in Deadpool's pre-release antics being shared
as entertainment rather than advertising.

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:08.740
This led to an unprecedented degree of organic
word of mouth, which likely played a huge

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:12.420
part in Deadpool's debut as the one of the
biggest R-rated openings of all time.

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:17.280
So what if Justice League has A-listers like
The Flash, Aquaman, and Wonder Woman?

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:21.700
No one cares about them or that movie; B-listers
are the new hot property.

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:27.359
After all, Deadpool was only as good as its
red-suited merc and his ensemble of backup

00:01:27.359 --> 00:01:32.090
dancers, which included beloved comic book
characters like… uhh hang on, let us check

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:35.780
our notes Weasel… and Negasonic Teenage
Warhead…

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:38.540
"Negasonic teenage — what the s---?!

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:41.160
That's the coolest name EVER!"

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:43.780
Okay, yeah, neither of these were fan favorites.

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:48.960
And while Colossus definitely has a following,
it'd be a serious stretch to call him an A-lister.

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:54.520
Really, the cast of this movie, from top to
bottom, was as B-list or below as it got and

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:56.160
that's probably why it worked so well.

00:01:56.530 --> 00:02:00.880
Virtually none of these characters had to
worry about living up to overwhelming audience

00:02:00.889 --> 00:02:05.619
expectations, meaning there was minimal pressure
and maximum freedom to adapt them however

00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:07.000
would best suit the film.

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:11.510
And that's exactly what Deadpool did, writing
them smartly and entertainingly enough to

00:02:11.510 --> 00:02:16.520
fit within the film's universe while simultaneously
maintaining their core comic book identities.

00:02:17.069 --> 00:02:21.900
For those reasons, the movie nailed building
its own team of budget Avengers, effectively

00:02:21.909 --> 00:02:26.060
proving to Hollywood that you don't need an
Iron Man or Wonder Woman to produce a moneymaker

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:28.280
loaded with franchise-starting characters.

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:29.280
"It's a big house.

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:30.770
It's funny that I only ever see two of you.

00:02:30.770 --> 00:02:33.640
It's almost like… the studio couldn't afford
another X-man."

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:39.340
If there's one movie that shines as a prime
example of what fans can accomplish when they

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:42.500
band together to create buzz on the internet,
it's Deadpool.

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:47.760
If not for the leaked test footage, we might
very well have never gotten a Deadpool solo

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:48.480
movie at all.

00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:55.220
But Fox felt compelled to dish out $58 million
which isn't even all that much in superhero

00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:58.980
movie money as a result of a lot of geeks
on the internet screaming about how much they

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:02.900
wanted some goofball in a red jumpsuit to
shoot people and say bad words.

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:04.240
What a time we live in.

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:06.220
"We all sort of said at the beginning, 'Someone
should leak it!

00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:07.230
Someone should leak it.'

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:09.800
So (???) the idea was (???) But I'm 70% sure
it wasn't me."

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:13.739
Though we've yet to see another brave test
footage leaker come out of the woodwork to

00:03:13.739 --> 00:03:19.209
make their hypothetical dream movie a studio-budgeted
reality, it doesn't change the fact that the

00:03:19.209 --> 00:03:23.380
Deadpool situation and the response by Fox
set a new precedent in the filmmaking world:

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:26.260
when the internet speaks, it can make real
change.

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:31.580
In the old days, Hollywood was a gated kingdom
where the true power brokers all wore suits

00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:32.860
and traded business cards.

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:37.560
Moving forward, we now know another party
has a voice at the decision-making table:

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:38.120
the fans.

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:40.440
It's just a matter of making enough noise.

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:47.020
A lot of films fall into the trap of being
overly sincere or, in more recent years, too

00:03:47.020 --> 00:03:47.520
self-aware.

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:52.280
Movies like Batman v Superman: Dawn of Justice
showed us what can happen when a movie takes

00:03:52.280 --> 00:03:53.910
itself far too seriously.

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:58.939
On the other end of the spectrum, if every
other minute of a film is littered with self-referential

00:03:58.940 --> 00:04:03.220
dialogue, then there's no room whatsoever
for real emotional investment by the audience.

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:08.480
Deadpool, the first and best of its meta-kind,
showed that it's possible to be extremely

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:11.900
self-aware but also legitimately emotional
at the same time.

00:04:12.220 --> 00:04:16.680
Deadpool tied most of its fourth-wall-breaking
antics to the main character's superhero alter

00:04:16.690 --> 00:04:22.000
ego, so that whenever Wade and Vanessa were
sharing an intimate, real beat, no cynical,

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:24.380
self-deprecating humor would crop up and ruin
the moment.

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:27.560
"Your life is officially waaay more f---ed
up than mine."

00:04:32.540 --> 00:04:33.060
"I love you."

00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:37.820
It was a smart balancing move that ensured
audiences would care about Wade and Vanessa's

00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:42.420
happiness and well-being, while also being
able to laugh at all of Deadpool's zany shenanigans.

00:04:43.480 --> 00:04:48.460
Prior to Deadpool, few thought that a movie
loaded with CGI gunfights, throwdowns, and

00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:51.440
explosions could ever operate as a functional
comedy.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:56.100
Guardians of the Galaxy was as close as any
superhero film had come to achieving that,

00:04:56.360 --> 00:04:59.260
and even that movie veered more into action-adventure
territory.

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:04.280
Deadpool went for the guffaws right from its
opening title sequence all the way 'til the

00:05:04.280 --> 00:05:05.460
very end of its credits.

00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:10.910
For the crowds who like raunchy Seth Rogen
comedies and Seth MacFarlane's comedic fare,

00:05:10.910 --> 00:05:15.130
this movie fit the bill and felt more like
a Marvel-flavored big-budget parody than an

00:05:15.130 --> 00:05:16.280
actual superhero movie.

00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:19.500
Deadpool's effects on the industry are readily
apparent.

00:05:19.940 --> 00:05:24.510
It might've come out later that same year,
but given the extensive reshoots Suicide Squad

00:05:24.510 --> 00:05:29.370
went through, one can venture a guess that
the movie's sudden influx of meta-humor might've

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:31.280
been the result of Deadpool's success.

00:05:31.540 --> 00:05:38.060
And just look at Shazam!, another keenly self-aware
superhero movie that, unlike its DCEU contemporaries,

00:05:38.060 --> 00:05:39.760
is actually, well… fun.

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:43.930
Who's to say what kind of tone it would've
aimed for without Deadpool to prove that a

00:05:43.930 --> 00:05:47.340
superhero movie doesn't have to be such a
brooding, mopey affair?

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:48.280
"Do you like what you see?"

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:49.220
"No.

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:54.520
You look like an avacado had sex with an older,
more disgusting avacado."

00:05:54.820 --> 00:05:55.320
"Yeah."

